        <body>
            <div xml:id="d485">

                <pb facs="0554" n="450" xml:id="pg450"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/4424: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Shanghai (Cunningham) to the
                        Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Shanghai</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-02-26T16:00:00+08:00">February 26, 1932—4
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received February 26—10:10 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>113. Continuing my daily report 111, February 25, 5 p.m.</p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>There is little or no change in the general situation since my report of
                        yesterday. Japanese have reported that this morning at 8:30 they raided the
                        Hangchow aerodrome with 25 planes during which time they are alleged to have
                        destroyed a number of Chinese planes. This report has not been
                        confirmed.</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>Mr. Jun Ke Choy, representative of the Mayor, states that the Chinese
                        Government at Nanking had been opposed to offensive measures until the
                        delivery of the Japanese ultimatum on February 19th. Now they are in favor
                        of an offensive campaign and that probably the next move would be to bring
                        down some two hundred thousand odd Chinese soldiers who have been fighting
                        the Communists in Kiangsi Province and turn that Province over to the
                        Communists. He said that it would be 2 or 3 weeks before they would arrive
                        and they would probably come by way of Chekiang. It is difficult to connect
                        this alleged act as a military operation in connection with defense of the
                        International Settlement.</item>
                </list>
                <p>Repeated to the Legation, Nanking. Copy to Minister.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Cunningham</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
